      "dom_id": "question-84984",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "A soccer coach must choose a defensive lineup for each of the $4$ defensive positions from a roster of $11$ defenders. How many potential starting defensive lineups are there?",
      "worked_solution": "The number of permutations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{P}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $11$ defenders, so $n=11.$ We want to select $4$ of those defenders to play in each of the $4$ positions, so we have $r=4.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different starting defensive lineups is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{11}{P}_{4} & =\\frac{11!}{(11-4)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{11!}{7!} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\cdot 10\\cdot 9\\cdot 8\\cdot \\cancel{7!}}{\\cancel{7!}} \\\\  & =11\\cdot 10\\cdot 9\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =7\\,920.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
